    "body": "> not sure what difference `diff=html` actually makes in git diff output, and if it’s what we really want. But I guess we can try it and see.\r\n\r\nI just took some time to actually try it, just making a trivial change locally.\r\n\r\nWithout `diff=html` I get this:\r\n\r\n```diff\r\n$ git diff source\r\ndiff --git a/source b/source\r\nindex 837db69..a8d6fab 100644\r\n--- a/source\r\n+++ b/source\r\n@@ -997,6 +997,7 @@ a.<span data-x=\"dom-hyperlink-protocol\">protocol</span> = 'https'; // change jus\r\n a.setAttribute('href', 'https://example.com/'); // change the content attribute directly</pre>\r\n\r\n   <p>Since DOM trees are used as the way to represent HTML documents when they are processed and\r\n+  foo\r\n   presented by implementations (especially interactive implementations like Web browsers), this\r\n   specification is mostly phrased in terms of DOM trees, instead of the markup described above.</p>\r\n```\r\n\r\nBut with `diff=html` I get this:\r\n\r\n```diff\r\n$ git diff source\r\ndiff --git a/source b/source\r\nindex 837db69..a8d6fab 100644\r\n--- a/source\r\n+++ b/source\r\n@@ -997,6 +997,7 @@ <h4 id=\"fingerprint-postMessage\">Cross-site communication</h4>\r\n a.setAttribute('href', 'https://example.com/'); // change the content attribute directly</pre>\r\n\r\n   <p>Since DOM trees are used as the way to represent HTML documents when they are processed and\r\n+  foo\r\n   presented by implementations (especially interactive implementations like Web browsers), this\r\n   specification is mostly phrased in terms of DOM trees, instead of the markup described above.</p>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThat is, in place of this not-very-useful “hunk header”:\r\n\r\n    @@ -997,6 +997,7 @@ a.<span data-x=\"dom-hyperlink-protocol\">protocol</span> = 'https'; // change jus\r\n\r\n…I instead get this:\r\n\r\n    @@ -997,6 +997,7 @@ <h4 id=\"fingerprint-postMessage\">Cross-site communication</h4>\r\n\r\nSo it seems `diff=html` causes `git diff` to look back up through the document to find the nearest `h1-h6` heading or maybe even the nearest element with an `id`.\r\n\r\nEither way, that seems pretty useful.\r\n",
